UID:36135-1793959200-1793964600@philosophy.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:Early Modern Philosophy Research Interest Group (Aman Sakhardande\, U of T)
DESCRIPTION:The Early Modern Philosophy Research Interest Group welcomes Aman Sakhardande\, a PhD student in the department working on phenomenology and 17th- and 18th-century European philosophy. Historically\, his research focuses on the work of Immanuel Kant\, Edmund Husserl\, and Martin Heidegger. Philosophically\, he is interested in what studying experience (cognitive\, perceptual\, practical\, etc.) can tell us about the world\, and in particular what it can tell us about the kinds of beings that we (humans) are. \nTalk Title\nIs Sympathy Selfish? Smith on the Grounds of Sympathy \nTalk Abstract\nIt is essential to Adam Smith’s Theory of Moral Sentiments that human beings use their imaginations to sympathize with one another. However\, it is not entirely clear what this imaginative process looks like. In a late passage\, Smith argues that this process not only involves imagining ourselves in the same situation as another person (projection)\, but also as being the same person as the other (identification). He does this to avoid the accusation that this imaginative process is selfish. However\, as some commentators have noticed\, this identification-based sympathy makes judgements of impropriety impossible. You judge a person to be (re)acting with impropriety when you imagine yourself in their situation\, and you don’t feel as they feel\, i.e.\, you don’t sympathize. Yet when we imaginatively identify with the other\, we must necessarily sympathize with them as well. This creates a dilemma: if Smith tries to save his view from selfishness\, his account of judgements of impropriety collapses; and if he tries to save his account of judgements of impropriety\, his view is vulnerable to accusations of selfishness. My aim in this paper is to resolve this dilemma by arguing against Smith’s premise that sympathy is selfish if it relies on projection. In the end\, I will propose the following interpretation of Smithian sympathy: insofar as sympathy is used for moral evaluation\, Smith ought to embrace projection-based sympathy; however\, in contexts where Smith is considering sympathy insofar as it is itself a moral act\, we can find space for Smith’s intuition that identification-based sympathy is the unselfish form of sympathizing.
URL:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/early-modern-philosophy-research-interest-group-aman-sahkardande-u-of-t/
LOCATION:Jackman Humanities Building\, Room 418\, 170 St. George Street\, Toronto\, Ontario\, M5R 2M8\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Graduate,St. George
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/Aman-resized.png
ORGANIZER;CN="Tarek Dika":MAILTO:tarek.dika@utoronto.ca
END:VEVENT
